# Chapter 8 — Sound

Your world moves now. A hero glides, a drop falls, a button pushes. But it all
happens in silence, and a game in silence is only half a game — the jump that
does not go *boing*, the coin that does not go *ding*, feels like a thing that
did not quite happen. Sound is the other half of feedback: it tells the player,
in the ear rather than the eye, that the world heard them.

This chapter turns on the console's voice. You will start a sound, ask whether
anything is sounding, stop it, and set how loud the whole console is. Four small
verbs, and your world stops being a silent movie.

## The console has a voice, and it starts quiet

Before you play anything, the console is silent. `audio/playing?` asks the sound
engine a plain question — *is any voice sounding right now?* — and at the start
the honest answer is no:

```motoi
(display (audio/playing?))
(newline)
```

```
#f
```

Nothing is playing, so `audio/playing?` is `#f`. This is your ear on the engine:
any time you want to know whether the console is making sound, you ask this verb.
It reads the *live* state of the audio system, not a guess — so it is the truth
you check your sound code against.

## Play a sound

`audio/play` starts a named sound and hands you back a **handle** — a little
token that stands for *this particular playing sound*, so you can refer to it
later (to stop it, say). Start one, then ask again:

```motoi
(define blip (audio/play 'blip))
(display (audio/playing?))
(newline)
```

```
#t
```

You named the sound `blip`, started it, and kept its handle in `blip`. Now
`audio/playing?` answers `#t` — a voice is live. On a real console that voice is
already reaching the speaker; here at the prompt the engine tracks it faithfully
even though no speaker is wired up, which is why the state still flips. **Start a
sound, and the console is sounding.** That is the whole first move.

## Stop it again

A sound you cannot stop is a sound that never ends. The handle you kept is how
you reach back and silence exactly that voice. `audio/halt` takes the handle and
stops it:

```motoi
(define blip (audio/play 'blip))
(display (audio/playing?))
(newline)
(audio/halt blip)
(display (audio/playing?))
(newline)
```

```
#t
#f
```

Watch the two answers. Right after `audio/play`, the console is sounding —
`#t`. You hand the handle to `audio/halt`, and the voice stops — the next
`audio/playing?` is `#f` again. This is the pair you will use constantly:
`audio/play` to begin, `audio/halt` to end, and the handle is the thread that
ties them together. Keep the handle whenever a sound might need stopping — a
looping engine hum, a held note, background music.

## Set how loud everything is

Individual sounds are one thing; the whole console has a master loudness knob.
`audio/master-volume` sets it, from `0` (silent) to `1` (full), and it affects
*every* voice at once:

```motoi
(audio/master-volume 0.5)
(display 'volume-set)
(newline)
```

```
volume-set
```

You turned the master volume to half. Like the screen verbs in Chapter 3, this
one does its work and we print a small marker — `volume-set` — so you can see
the line ran. Master volume is the one dial that touches all sound, which makes
it the right place for a mute toggle or a settings slider: turn it to `0` and
the whole console goes quiet without stopping a single voice, then back to `1`
and everything you started is audible again.

## What you just proved

You gave your world a voice. You asked the engine whether it was sounding and
got the truth (`#f` at rest), started a named sound and watched `audio/playing?`
flip to `#t`, stopped exactly that sound with its handle, and set the master
loudness for the whole console. Every program ran and the audio state changed
exactly as the verbs promised.

A single sound is a *ding*. What turns dings into music is *timing* — playing
them on a steady pulse, on the beat. That pulse is the next chapter, and it is
the same idea as the tick from Chapter 5, now counting in music instead of
frames.

> **Tip**
>
> Always keep the handle `audio/play` gives you if the sound might need
> stopping. A quick one-shot — a coin *ding* — you can start and forget. But
> anything that loops or holds (music, an engine, a siren) needs its handle
> saved, or you will have no way to reach back and call `audio/halt` on it.

> **Warning**
>
> At the prompt there is no speaker, so you will not *hear* these sounds — but
> the engine still tracks them, which is why `audio/playing?` flips to `#t` and
> back. Do not take silence at the prompt to mean your sound code is broken;
> check `audio/playing?` instead of your ears. On a live console the same calls
> reach the speaker. Appendix E lays out the synth and audio engine in full.

